            <div class=3D"region region-content">
        <h1 class=3D"page-header">
<span>William Blake: Paradise Lost</span>
</h1>

 =20
<article data-history-node-id=3D"7747176" class=3D"exhibitions full clearfi=
x">

 =20
   =20

 =20
  <div class=3D"content">
   =20
   =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-field-display-date field--type-=
string field--label-hidden field--item">February 26 through May 23, 2027</d=
iv>
     =20
      <div class=3D"field field--name-field-image field--type-image field--=
label-hidden field--items">
              <div class=3D"field--item">  <img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"htt=
ps://www.themorgan.org/sites/default/files/exhibitions/Blake_Temptation_Fal=
l_Eve_MFABoston_SC513757.jpg" width=3D"463" height=3D"596" alt=3D"" class=
=3D"img-responsive copyprevention-processed">

</div>
          </div>
 =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-body field--type-text-with-summ=
ary field--label-hidden field--item"><p>To mark the two hundredth anniversa=
ry of the death of visionary artist and poet William Blake (1757=E2=80=9318=
27), this exhibition will be the first in-depth exploration of Blake=E2=80=
=99s profound and persistent engagement with England=E2=80=99s canonic poet=
, John Milton (1608=E2=80=931674). Milton, who predates Blake by more than =
a century, was a statesman, polemicist, and author of the biblical epic <em=
>Paradise Lost.</em> Yet the legacy of his writings and ideas coursed throu=
gh Blake=E2=80=99s imagination from youth to old age, taking form in vision=
s, poetry, and the astonishing body of watercolor drawings he produced in t=
he second half of his career.</p><p>This exhibition will reunite Blake=E2=
=80=99s most significant works inspired by Milton, alongside selections by =
his contemporaries, including Henri Fuseli and John Flaxman. For the first =
time in more than a quarter century, visitors will gain unprecedented acces=
s to Blake=E2=80=99s rarely seen Miltonic watercolors, illuminated books, a=
nd paintings, drawn from renowned American and British collections and the =
Morgan=E2=80=99s own formidable holdings by Blake and Milton.</p><p>After 1=
800, Milton=E2=80=99s poetry rivaled the Bible as Blake=E2=80=99s dominant =
literary inspiration. Blake=E2=80=99s relationship to the poet and thinker,=
 however, was complex. He confronted and wrestled with Milton=E2=80=99s wri=
tings and beliefs. This struggle helped to solidify Blake=E2=80=99s own ide=
as, yielding his most personal and provocative achievements as a mature art=
ist. Blake=E2=80=99s efforts culminated in his own ambitious epic <em>Milto=
n, a Poem</em> (1804-1811) and the extraordinary sets of watercolor drawing=
s illustrating Milton=E2=80=99s major works: the early lyric poem =E2=80=9C=
On the Morning of Christ=E2=80=99s Nativity=E2=80=9D (1629); the companion =
poems <em>L=E2=80=99Allegro</em> and <em>Il Penseroso </em>(c. 1631); the m=
asque <em>Comus</em> (1634); and the epics <em>Paradise Lost</em> (1667) an=
d <em>Paradise Regained</em> (1671).</p></div>
     =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-field-credit-line field--type-t=
ext-long field--label-hidden field--item"><p>Organized by Jennifer Tonkovic=
h, Eugene and Clare Thaw Curator, Department of Drawings and Prints, and Sh=
eelagh Bevan, Andrew W. Mellon Curator, Department of Printed Books and Bin=
dings.</p><p><em>William Blake: Paradise Lost</em> is made possible by an a=
nonymous donor, in memory of Melvin R. Seiden, and by Rudy L. Ruggles, Jr. =
and Andrew C. Ruggles. Generous support is provided by the Robert Lehman Fu=
nd for Exhibitions, the William Randolph Hearst Fund for Scholarly Research=
 and Exhibitions, and the Lucy Ricciardi Family Exhibition Fund, with addit=
ional support from Robert Dance. It is a program of the Morgan Drawing Inst=
itute.</p></div>
     =20
            <div class=3D"field field--name-field-image-caption field--type=
-text-long field--label-hidden field--item"><p>William Blake (English, 1757=
=E2=80=931827). <em>The Temptation and Fall of Eve</em> (Illustration to Mi=
lton's "Paradise Lost"), 1808. Black ink and watercolor over graphite on pa=
per. Sheet: 49.7 =C3=97 38.7 cm (19 9/16 =C3=97 15 1/4 in.). Museum of Fine=
 Arts, Boston, Museum purchase with funds donated by contribution, 90.99. P=
hotograph =C2=A9 2026 Museum of Fine Arts, Boston</p></div>
     =20
  </div>

</article>

